      "section": "P2",
      "question_number": 4,
      "question_text": "4 A student attempts to answer the following question. What is the largest number of consecutive odd integers that are all prime? The student’s attempt is as follows: I There are two consecutive odd integers that are prime (for example: 17, 19). II Any three consecutive odd integers can be written in the form n – 2, n, n + 2 for some n. III If n is one more than a multiple of 3, then n + 2 is a multiple of 3. IV If n is two more than a multiple of 3, then n – 2 is a multiple of 3. V The only other possibility is that n is a multiple of 3. VI In each case, one of the integers is a multiple of 3, so not prime. VII Therefore the largest number of consecutive odd integers that are all prime is two. Which of the following best describes this attempt?",
      "has_diagram": false,
      "options": {
        "A": "It is completely correct.",
